#v(0.5cm)

#emph[
  On-time student graduation is one of the primary indicators in evaluating higher education performance and accreditation. Graduation delays often go undetected at an early stage because academic monitoring processes are still conducted manually and separately. This study aims to design and implement a predictive model for on-time student graduation using machine learning algorithms based on academic history data. The dataset utilized includes attributes such as Grade Point Average (GPA), total credits taken, lecture attendance rates, and student demographic backgrounds from the last three cohorts. The research stages consist of data preprocessing, feature selection, training and testing data splitting, and model training using Random Forest and Support Vector Machine (SVM) algorithms. Experimental results indicate that the Random Forest algorithm achieved the highest accuracy of 92.4% with an F1-Score of 0.91 compared to the baseline method. This model is subsequently integrated into a web-based information system as an early warning tool for academic advisors to conduct more proactive and measurable student mentoring.
]

